<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="jc-number-rich-investigations">
<title>Number Investigations and CBA Ideas</title>
<introduction><p>These optional investigations build on arithmetic, factors, fractions and patterns. Choose a question that interests you, use paper, pen and a calculator, and keep a record of your thinking. A spreadsheet can help with larger searches. The starting questions are suggestions, not compulsory CBA assignments.</p><p>For a CBA, develop your own question and method, record evidence, explain conclusions and reflect on limitations. See <url href="https://curriculumonline.ie/junior-cycle/junior-cycle-subjects/mathematics/examples-of-student-work/" visual="official NCCA examples of student work"/>.</p></introduction>
<section xml:id="number-investigations-bank"><title>Twenty Investigations</title>
<investigation xml:id="number-investigation-perfect"><title>Perfect Numbers</title><p>List the proper positive factors of 6, 12, 20 and 28. Add them. Investigate all numbers from 1 to 100. Can you find another number equal to the sum of its proper factors?</p><p><em>Develop your investigation:</em> Use factor pairs and a table with columns for number, proper factors and sum. What changes if you include the number itself?</p><p>Record your evidence, state a conjecture, test it with new examples and explain what you can conclude.</p></investigation>
<investigation xml:id="number-investigation-abundant"><title>Abundant and Deficient Numbers</title><p>Compare each number from 1 to 50 with the sum of its proper positive factors. Classify it as deficient, perfect or abundant.</p><p><em>Develop your investigation:</em> Are all odd numbers deficient? Extend your search beyond 50 and investigate 945.</p><p>Record your evidence, state a conjecture, test it with new examples and explain what you can conclude.</p></investigation>
<investigation xml:id="number-investigation-amicable"><title>Amicable Numbers</title><p>Find the proper-factor sum of 220, then of 284. Search for other pairs using a spreadsheet or calculator.</p><p><em>Develop your investigation:</em> Explain why a perfect number is different from an amicable pair of distinct numbers.</p><p>Record your evidence, state a conjecture, test it with new examples and explain what you can conclude.</p></investigation>
<investigation xml:id="number-investigation-divisors"><title>The Divisor Challenge</title><p>Find the number or numbers from 1 to 100 with the greatest number of positive divisors. Record factor pairs systematically.</p><p><em>Develop your investigation:</em> Compare prime factorisations of the leading candidates. Can you predict a good candidate below 200?</p><p>Record your evidence, state a conjecture, test it with new examples and explain what you can conclude.</p></investigation>
<investigation xml:id="number-investigation-squares"><title>Squares and Odd Divisor Counts</title><p>Count the positive divisors of 1, 4, 9, 16, 25 and several non-squares.</p><p><em>Develop your investigation:</em> Pair each divisor with its complementary factor. Explain what happens at the square root.</p><p>Record your evidence, state a conjecture, test it with new examples and explain what you can conclude.</p></investigation>
<investigation xml:id="number-investigation-primes"><title>Prime Number Patterns</title><p>Use a hundred square to eliminate multiples of 2, 3, 5 and 7, retaining each prime itself. Compare prime counts in successive blocks of ten.</p><p><em>Develop your investigation:</em> Why is it enough to test primes up to the square root when checking a positive integer greater than one?</p><p>Record your evidence, state a conjecture, test it with new examples and explain what you can conclude.</p></investigation>
<investigation xml:id="number-investigation-semiprimes"><title>Products of Two Primes</title><p>List numbers up to 100 that are products of exactly two primes, counting repetition. Include 46 and 49.</p><p><em>Develop your investigation:</em> Separate squares of primes from products of distinct primes. Compare the numbers of positive divisors.</p><p>Record your evidence, state a conjecture, test it with new examples and explain what you can conclude.</p></investigation>
<investigation xml:id="number-investigation-sphenic"><title>Products of Three Distinct Primes</title><p>Construct numbers using three different primes, beginning with 30 and 42. List their divisors.</p><p><em>Develop your investigation:</em> Predict the divisor count and explain it by choosing whether to include each prime.</p><p>Record your evidence, state a conjecture, test it with new examples and explain what you can conclude.</p></investigation>
<investigation xml:id="number-investigation-three"><title>Crossing Out Digits</title><p>Test divisibility by three for 732, 74295, 804217 and numbers of your own choice. Cross out 0, 3, 6 and 9, then pairs summing to 9.</p><p><em>Develop your investigation:</em> Compare the remaining digit sum with the original. Explain why removing a multiple of three cannot change divisibility by three.</p><p>Record your evidence, state a conjecture, test it with new examples and explain what you can conclude.</p></investigation>
<investigation xml:id="number-investigation-eleven"><title>The Rule for Eleven</title><p>Investigate 121, 352, 1232 and 9185 by subtracting alternate digit sums. Check each using division.</p><p><em>Develop your investigation:</em> Invent four-digit examples that pass the test. Does reversing a number preserve divisibility by eleven?</p><p>Record your evidence, state a conjecture, test it with new examples and explain what you can conclude.</p></investigation>
<investigation xml:id="number-investigation-times-eleven"><title>Mental Multiplication by Eleven</title><p>Multiply 23, 45, 72, 89 and 234 by eleven. Compare the original digits with the digits of the product.</p><p><em>Develop your investigation:</em> Develop a method that handles carrying. Explain it using multiplication by ten plus the original number.</p><p>Record your evidence, state a conjecture, test it with new examples and explain what you can conclude.</p></investigation>
<investigation xml:id="number-investigation-recurring"><title>Repeating Decimals</title><p>Use long division to investigate the decimal expansions of unit fractions with denominators 2 to 20. Record repeating blocks exactly rather than relying only on a calculator display.</p><p><em>Develop your investigation:</em> Track the remainders. What happens when a remainder repeats or becomes zero?</p><p>Record your evidence, state a conjecture, test it with new examples and explain what you can conclude.</p></investigation>
<investigation xml:id="number-investigation-sevenths"><title>The Mysterious Sevenths</title><p>Find the decimal expansions of 1/7 through 6/7. Record their repeating blocks and compare them.</p><p><em>Develop your investigation:</em> Describe the cyclic pattern and investigate whether a similar pattern occurs with denominator 13.</p><p>Record your evidence, state a conjecture, test it with new examples and explain what you can conclude.</p></investigation>
<investigation xml:id="number-investigation-terminating"><title>Predicting Terminating Decimals</title><p>Classify reduced unit fractions with denominators 2 to 30 as terminating or recurring. Record the prime factorisation of each denominator.</p><p><em>Develop your investigation:</em> Form a conjecture involving 2 and 5. Test non-unit fractions after simplifying them.</p><p>Record your evidence, state a conjecture, test it with new examples and explain what you can conclude.</p></investigation>
<investigation xml:id="number-investigation-nines"><title>Recurring Nines</title><p>Compare 0.9, 0.99 and 0.999 with 1. Calculate the gaps and describe their pattern.</p><p><em>Develop your investigation:</em> Let x be the infinite recurring decimal. Compare 10x and x. Explain why finite approximations differ from the infinite decimal.</p><p>Record your evidence, state a conjecture, test it with new examples and explain what you can conclude.</p></investigation>
<investigation xml:id="number-investigation-egyptian"><title>Egyptian Fractions</title><p>Find different ways to express 1/2, 2/3 and 3/4 as sums of distinct unit fractions. Draw fraction bars to check your results.</p><p><em>Develop your investigation:</em> Use the identity 1/n = 1/(n+1) + 1/(n(n+1)) to create more representations.</p><p>Record your evidence, state a conjecture, test it with new examples and explain what you can conclude.</p></investigation>
<investigation xml:id="number-investigation-lockers"><title>The Hundred Lockers</title><p>Begin with 100 closed lockers. On pass k, change the state of each locker whose number is a multiple of k. Simulate the first 20 lockers before extending.</p><p><em>Develop your investigation:</em> Connect the number of changes to the number of divisors. Predict which lockers remain open.</p><p>Record your evidence, state a conjecture, test it with new examples and explain what you can conclude.</p></investigation>
<investigation xml:id="number-investigation-coins"><title>Coin Combinations</title><p>Using only 4-unit and 7-unit tokens, list amounts that can and cannot be made with nonnegative quantities. Investigate up to 40.</p><p><em>Develop your investigation:</em> Find a run of four consecutive attainable amounts. Explain why every later amount is then attainable by adding 4.</p><p>Record your evidence, state a conjecture, test it with new examples and explain what you can conclude.</p></investigation>
<investigation xml:id="number-investigation-consecutive"><title>Sums of Consecutive Numbers</title><p>Try expressing integers from 1 to 50 as sums of at least two consecutive positive integers. Organise results by number of terms.</p><p><em>Develop your investigation:</em> Investigate powers of two and odd numbers. Explain why every odd number greater than one is the sum of two consecutive positive integers.</p><p>Record your evidence, state a conjecture, test it with new examples and explain what you can conclude.</p></investigation>
<investigation xml:id="number-investigation-reverse"><title>Reversing Digits</title><p>Choose two-digit numbers with unequal nonzero digits. Reverse the digits and subtract the smaller number from the larger. Record the results.</p><p><em>Develop your investigation:</em> Represent the original number as 10a+b and the reversal as 10b+a. Explain the connection to multiples of nine.</p><p>Record your evidence, state a conjecture, test it with new examples and explain what you can conclude.</p></investigation>
</section>
<section xml:id="number-investigations-cba-models"><title>How an Investigation Might Develop</title>
<p>These are fictional teaching illustrations, not actual student submissions or reports to copy. Your own investigation should reflect your own decisions and evidence.</p>
<example><title>Model A: Perfect Numbers</title><p>Question: Are there any perfect numbers between 1 and 100? A student defines a proper factor as a positive factor smaller than the number, and records factor pairs without double-counting square roots.</p><table><title>Extract from an illustrative investigation</title><tabular><row><cell>Number</cell><cell>Proper factors</cell><cell>Sum</cell></row><row><cell>6</cell><cell>1, 2, 3</cell><cell>6</cell></row><row><cell>12</cell><cell>1, 2, 3, 4, 6</cell><cell>16</cell></row><row><cell>28</cell><cell>1, 2, 4, 7, 14</cell><cell>28</cell></row></tabular></table><p>A complete search from 1 to 100 would find only 6 and 28. The extract alone is not evidence that there are no others; the student must retain the full search. A possible extension is to investigate how far apart perfect numbers occur. The student should explain the limitations of a finite search.</p></example>
<example><title>Model B: Terminating or Recurring?</title><p>Question: Can I predict whether a fraction has a terminating decimal without dividing? A student compares reduced fractions, records denominator factors and uses long division to verify classifications.</p><p>For example, <m>1/8=0.125</m>, <m>1/20=0.05</m>, and <m>1/6=0.1\overline{6}</m>. A conjecture is that a reduced fraction terminates exactly when its denominator has no prime factors other than 2 or 5. Test <m>3/12=1/4</m> to discover why simplification matters.</p><p>A justification is that a terminating decimal can be written with denominator a power of ten, and powers of ten have only the prime factors 2 and 5. Conversely a denominator made from powers of 2 and 5 divides a sufficiently large power of ten.</p></example>
<example><title>Model C: The Locker Problem</title><p>Question: Which lockers stay open after every pass? A student makes a table for 12 lockers, recording open and closed states after each pass. The open lockers at the end are 1, 4 and 9.</p><p>Rather than merely predicting the pattern for 100 lockers, the student explains it: locker n changes state once for each positive divisor of n. Divisors come in pairs except at a square root that is an integer. Therefore exactly the square-numbered lockers have an odd number of changes and remain open. Predict 10 open lockers out of 100 and verify by simulation.</p></example>
</section>
<section xml:id="number-investigations-report"><title>Writing Your Own Mathematical Investigation</title><p>Define a clear question. Choose and explain your strategy. Record your mathematical work systematically. Interpret the findings in relation to the question, distinguish a conjecture from a proof, and explain what you would investigate next. Your report may include tables, graphs, diagrams, calculations and reflections. Do not copy the illustrative reports.</p></section>
</chapter>